<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1970 CE · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Zaydi kingdom of the Hamid al-Din imams, which held North Yemen in deliberate isolation from 1918 until the 1962 revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a> Muhammad Hamid al-Din had led the Zaydi resistance against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a> and gained autonomy in 1911. When the Ottoman garrisons left after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1918, he entered Sanaa as ruler of an independent state, taking the title al-Mutawakkil.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> ruled as religious and political heads, with power concentrated in the family and in hostages taken from the tribes to guarantee loyalty. Yahya kept <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> isolated, built a small army and claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">British Aden Protectorate</a>. A war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a> in 1934 lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jizan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-taif">Treaty of Ta'if</a>. Yahya was assassinated in 1948, and his son Ahmad ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ahmad died in September 1962. A week later army officers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-al-sallal">Abdullah al-Sallal</a> overthrew his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr">Muhammad al-Badr</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a>. Royalists backed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a> fought republicans backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> until a settlement in 1970.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen.jpg" alt="Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Urmas (SVG file) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Mutawakkilite_Kingdom_of_Yemen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1970 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yahya Muhammad Hamid al-Din, Ahmad bin Yahya, Muhammad al-Badr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zaydi Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Yemen, Mutawakkilite Kingdom, Hamid al-Din Imamate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen" aria-label="Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5886 1672 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6206 1928L6207 1863M6206 1928L6207 1863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6207 1863h0M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6206 1928h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6206 1928h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
